# GitHub issue #22

**Repository:** alexisNorthcoders/snake-colyseus
**URL:** https://github.com/alexisNorthcoders/snake-colyseus/issues/22
**State:** OPEN
**Labels:** ready-for-agent
**Title:** vs-bot room with a bot that plays

## Body
## What to build

A room created with a `vsBot` option is a private match against one server-side bot. The room locks at creation so matchmaking never places a stranger into it. The bot is seated in the lobby next to the human, and when the round starts it moves every tick like any other snake: it can eat, grow, die and win.

The bot is a virtual player inside the room: no socket and no client. It gets a new synced `isBot` flag on `Player`, a synthetic id that can't collide with a session id, and its own name and colours. Each tick, just before the snakes move, the room builds a plain-data snapshot of what a human player could see and asks the bot for a direction. That seam is the whole point of this ticket:

```ts
decide(view: BotView, profile: BotProfile): Direction   // pure: no Schema objects, no room, no internal RNG
BotView = { grid, self: { head, body, movedDirection }, others: { head, body, isDead }[], food: { x, y, type, score }[] }
```

The policy behind the seam is a deliberately dumb placeholder: go straight, and turn only when the next cell is fatal. A follow-up issue (rookie bot policy) replaces it.

A reminder of why it's shaped this way: `spawnCells` throws above 4 snakes and `maxClients` is already 4. A bot must therefore only ever exist in a locked vs-bot room (1 human + 1 bot). It must never be added to a room that can still take humans.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Creating a room with `vsBot` locks it at creation; `joinOrCreate` from another client never lands in it
- [ ] The room seats exactly one bot `Player` with `isBot = true`, a synthetic id, a name and colours, visible in the lobby state
- [ ] `isBot` is a synced field on `Player`, defaulting to false for humans
- [ ] Each tick, the bot's direction comes from `decide()` over a plain `BotView` snapshot, applied just before the movement phase and under the same no-reversal rule as a human `move`
- [ ] If `decide()` throws, the bot keeps its current direction and the round carries on
- [ ] Nothing addressed to a client (pong, broadcasts to a session) targets the bot's id
- [ ] Human dies first → the round ends with the bot as winner; bot dies first → the human wins. `aliveCount` and rankings are correct in both cases
- [ ] The human leaving mid-round ends the round and the room disposes (the bot doesn't keep it alive)
- [ ] Integration test: a human+bot round starts, ticks and ends with sane rankings; the existing suite stays green

## Blocked by

None (can start immediately)

